      </example>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!-- END CLICK TO REVEAL NO TEXTBOX -->
      <!-- BEGIN CLICK TO REVEAL NO TEXTBOX -->
      <!--Q#8-->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Draw a number line, including a scale, so that the point marked with a circle represents the solution to the inequality: \( 7.50h + \frac {50}{4}h &gt; 18h + 33 \).
        <figure><media alt="A blank number line is shown with an open circle on a point in the middle.&#10;        "><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_09_05.svg"/></media></figure></para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
            <figure>
              <media alt="A number line is shown with an open circle on a point in the middle. The number is shaded to the right of the open circle.">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_09_06.jpg"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!-- END CLICK TO REVEAL NO TEXTBOX -->
      <!-- BEGIN TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
      <!--Q#9-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Explain how you know.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para> Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.</para>
          <list>
            <item>Inequality 1 shows the pay at the hotel \( (18h+33) \) being greater than the pay at the restaurant \( 7.50h+ \frac{50}{4} \). Earlier, we found out that the hotel job is better when the student works less than 16.5 hours. Graph B shows \( h&lt;16.5 \). This means Graph B shows the solution to Inequality 1.</item>
            <item>If we substitute a value greater than 16.5 for \( h \), we have a true statement for Inequality 2 and a false statement for Inequality 1. This means \( h&gt;16.5 \) is a solution to Inequality 2 but not to Inequality 1.</item>
            <item>If we substitute a value less than 16.5 for \( h \), we have a true statement for Inequality 1 but not for Inequality 2. So \( h&lt;16.5 \) is a solution to Inequality 1.</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!-- END TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
    </section>
  </content>
